id: pm-jn1x30
title: "GH-1039: graph impact discards dependency direction and makes the direction selector inert"
description: "The public graph impact primitive walks dependency edges as an undirected component and echoes incoming/outgoing/both without changing traversal, so blast radius includes unrelated provenance branches."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 1
tags[8]: agent-ux,"area:graph","area:sdk",context-integrity,directionality,github-1039,impact,regression
created_at: "2026-08-16T05:11:38.234Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-16T07:05:16.534Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-16T06:16:13.224Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-16T06:16:13.224Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 420
acceptance_criteria: "Define incoming, outgoing, and both semantics once in the SDK graph traversal contract; preserve semantic orientation for dependency and inverse relationship kinds; make blast radius return only transitive dependents by default or document a compatible explicit default; keep CLI, MCP, PmClient, contracts, cache keys, and cost receipts aligned; add unit, integration, adversarial spanning-node, packed-consumer, and negative-control tests; document migration and token-cost effects."
goal: project management = context management
objective: Make graph impact answer directional invalidation and provenance questions without crossing unrelated branches.
value: Agents and schedulers can re-run only work that actually derives from a changed item.
impact: "One spanning node can collapse a workspace into a false blast radius, wasting compute and poisoning context ranking."
outcome: "Directed, explainable impact traversal is shared by SDK, CLI, and MCP with trustworthy direction and cost receipts."
why_now: GitHub #1039 proves all three direction values are byte-identical and over-report two unrelated nodes in a five-node graph.
parent: pm-7ob5
risk: high
confidence: high
severity: high
environment: pm graph impact on 2026.8.15
repro_steps: "Create two independent dependency branches joined by one transfer item, then run graph impact from the first root with incoming, outgoing, and both."
resolution: Impact traversal follows stored source-to-target direction and computes both mode as the union of direction-locked closures with bounded pagination.
expected_result: "Incoming returns transitive dependents, outgoing returns transitive prerequisites, and both returns their explicit union without reversing edges mid-path."
actual_result: All directions return the same undirected connected component and identical inspected-edge counts.
